### Before the session: the Job Board

Expeditions are posted on the [Job Board](jobs.html). Each listing gives what is known: the objective, a briefing, who posted it, when it is scheduled and how many crew it needs. Players sign up for the jobs that interest them, and the crew is whoever signed up for that job.

- Players choose which expeditions to pursue. Nobody is assigned.
- Not every job has to be completed. Some will be abandoned, some will fail, and some will be overtaken by events.
- Places that were already explored can be revisited, for example to finish a survey, recover what was left behind or follow a new lead.

### 1. Preparation

The crew reviews what is known and decides how to approach the job. This can include:

- reading reports and Gate records in the [Archive](archive.html);
- getting your equipment ready: choose your **Active Pack**, check what is left inside it, then pick the additional Gear you bring within its **Carry Limit**, buying anything missing from the [Marketplace](marketplace.html) (see [Coins, Marketplace & Personal Stash](game.html#post-coins-marketplace-and-personal-stash));
- discussing the environment and likely dangers, starting with the Gate's Domain (see [Domains](game.html#post-domains));
- using the settlement's Facilities and Capabilities (see the [Outpost Sheet](outpost.html)).

Preparation should matter, but it shouldn't turn into a long shopping trip or endless planning. Decide what matters, then go. Something you leave behind can still turn up later, at a cost (see [Coins, Marketplace & Personal Stash](game.html#post-coins-marketplace-and-personal-stash)).

What Endros has built shapes the options here. Finished Projects, completed Research, new Marketplace items, Facilities and Resource properties people have discovered can all give a crew options the last crew didn't have.

### 2. Exploration

This is the main part of the expedition. The crew enters the Gate, learns how the place works, deals with threats, pursues the objective and recovers whatever they can.

Fighting is one tool among many. Observing, experimenting and understanding the environment matter just as much. A Gate whose rules you understand is far less dangerous than one you only know how to fight.

Somewhere in each Gate is a **Core**. Recovering the Core collapses the Gate. No Core may be moved without the [[gate-evaluation-committee|Gate Evaluation Committee]]'s explicit authorization, and closing a Gate that supplies Endros is a public decision: its established supplies start to run down (see [Resources](game.html#post-resources)). Even with authorization, the Gate is gone for good, so the crew may need to decide whether that is the goal or a step too far.

### 3. Return

The expedition is resolved and its results are recorded:

- discoveries and observations;
- recovered Resources and artifacts, with names for any new Resources you discovered (you name what you find; see [Resources](game.html#post-resources));
- Cores, if one was recovered;
- consequences of the expedition, for the crew and for Endros;
- new Research and Project opportunities;
- changes to Endros;
- rewards;
- Downtime gained (see [Downtime](game.html#post-downtime));
- Coins refreshed from Resources, then reward Coins added (see [Coins, Marketplace & Personal Stash](game.html#post-coins-marketplace-and-personal-stash)).

Return feeds straight into the Settlement Loop. What the crew brings back becomes material for Research, Projects and new jobs.

### Why reports matter

Knowledge stays in the world after a crew goes home. A session's report in the Archive tells the next crew what to expect, what to bring and what not to touch. Writing down what you learned is a way of helping players you may never share a table with.

### The game and the company

These are two different things that share a name.

- **Nowhere Expeditions, the game,** is the campaign you're playing. Every player takes part in it, whatever their faction.
- **Nowhere Expeditions, the company,** is a business operating in Endros. Only characters who chose it as their Recruitment Faction came through it.

The [recruitment notice](game.html#post-game-listing) on the Announcements tab is one of the company's advertisements.

### Lore

Nowhere Expeditions specializes in finding people abroad who are willing to move to the edge of the known world, and in getting them there. For its recruits it can arrange:

- transportation to Endros;
- relocation assistance and temporary or subsidized housing;
- help finding employment;
- expedition registration and other administrative support;
- introductions to employers and expedition organizers;
- general help settling in.

None of this is charity. The company profits from the people it brings in, for example through commissions, placement fees or a share of expedition rewards. The exact terms depend on each recruit's contract.

For a sponsored character, the company is somewhere between a benefactor and a business partner. It has an office to visit, agents who know your name, and paperwork with your signature on it.

### Recruitment Extra

> **Relocation Package**
> After each expedition you take part in, gain **+1 Coin**.

This isn't the company handing you a coin each time. It stands for what your arrangement saves you: subsidized housing, lower living costs, better contract terms. Add it after your Resources refresh, together with any other rewards (see [Coins, Marketplace & Personal Stash](game.html#post-coins-marketplace-and-personal-stash)).

### Expedition Pack

{{expedition-pack}}

### Expectations

The company has expectations of its recruits, such as:

- staying active and taking on expeditions;
- honoring the terms of their contract;
- appearing in promotional material;
- letting the company take its agreed commissions.

These are hooks for roleplay, and possible Compels. They are not automatic penalties.

### Lore

The University of Verna runs laboratories in Endros. They are listed among the Outpost's Research facilities on the [Outpost Sheet](outpost.html). With them come archives, specialist equipment, academic contacts and a great deal of institutional expertise.

The Gates and the materials found beyond them are the only known source of magic in the world, and the University intends to understand them. To do that it brings people to the Outpost:

- researchers, archaeologists and historians;
- engineers and natural philosophers;
- physicians;
- other relevant scholars.

You don't need to be a traditional academic to be sponsored. Field specialists, technicians, assistants and unusual experts all qualify, as long as the University thinks your work is worth supporting.

### Recruitment Extra

> **Institutional Access**
> Once per between-expedition period, when you make a **Research Project Action**, gain **+1 additional Progress** if the University's laboratories, archives, specialists or equipment could reasonably assist you.

The bonus needs a reason in the fiction. When you describe your approach, say how the University helps: a microscope in its laboratories, a record in its archive, a colleague who has seen something similar. A "between-expedition period" is the time between one expedition and the next. See [Research](game.html#post-research).

### Expedition Pack

{{expedition-pack}}

### Expectations

The University may expect its people to:

- submit their research;
- share samples;
- publish their findings;
- contribute to academic projects;
- assist other University personnel.

These are hooks for roleplay, and possible Compels. They are not automatic penalties.

### Lore

Independents reached Endros without meaningful sponsorship from a major institution. They might have:

- paid their own way;
- arrived through personal contacts;
- worked their passage aboard a ship;
- used family money or their own savings;
- followed an unusual opportunity;
- come through a smaller organization that offers no real ongoing support.

Being Independent isn't the "no faction" option. It's a choice for characters whose identity is their freedom: no contract, no institution, no one else's agenda. Their time and their work are their own.

### Recruitment Extra

> **Self-Directed**
> Once per between-expedition period, when you make a **Personal Project Action**, gain **+1 additional Progress**.

A **Personal Project** is one whose main benefit when completed belongs to your character, such as:

- creating an item for your Personal Stash;
- modifying your own equipment;
- restoring a personal artifact;
- developing a personal invention;
- a similar individual undertaking.

Self-Directed doesn't apply to Public Projects, Marketplace Projects, Facilities or other Outpost development. A "between-expedition period" is the time between one expedition and the next. See [Projects](game.html#post-projects) and [Personal Stash & Crafting](game.html#post-personal-stash-and-crafting).

### Expedition Pack

{{expedition-pack}}

### Expectations

Independents have no formal obligations to any institution.

They also don't have the contacts and support that come with a sponsor. Nobody arranges their introductions or opens doors for them. Any help they get, they find themselves.

### What Research is for

Start a Research Project to:

- understand an artifact;
- investigate a Gate phenomenon;
- reveal a Resource's Hidden Functions (see [Resources](game.html#post-resources));
- develop a new spell Form (see [Spell Innovation](game.html#post-spell-innovation));
- analyze a creature or environment;
- answer a question about the setting;
- unlock new Project opportunities;
- unlock new information in the Archive.

### How it works

Use the normal [Projects](game.html#post-projects) rules: spend 1 Downtime, describe your approach, choose a Skill (often Lore or Investigate, but the approach decides), roll and gain Progress. Endros's **Research** Capability can support you when its laboratories, scholars or records help with what you are doing: spend 1 extra Downtime on the action to add its rating (see [Outpost Capabilities](game.html#post-outpost-capabilities)).

Research often has Knowledge or Resource Requirements, like a sample, a second specimen or someone who can read the script. Its Complications often lead to an expedition objective. See [Project Requirements & Complications](game.html#post-project-requirements-and-complications).

### What you learn

Finishing Research doesn't always give a complete answer. It may instead:

- reveal **partial** information;
- **rule out** incorrect theories;
- reveal a **hidden property**;
- raise a **new question** that becomes the next Research Project;
- give **preparation information** for a future Gate.

> **Example.** Research into a humming shard recovered on a recent expedition finishes. The result: the shard isn't a weapon, it hums louder near running water, and it has a Hidden Function, now revealed as `Resonate`. Why it reacts to water is still unknown, which is a good next Research Project.

### Where the results go

What you learn becomes part of the shared world. Completed Research may add new [Archive](archive.html) entries or make hidden information visible, so every crew can use it, not only the researcher.

### The Domains

{{domain-list}}

Every Domain, with its Gates and Resources, is in [Discoveries](discoveries.html#domains).

Ordinary trade materials from the wider world, such as Bloom Iron from the Holds or Purified Alum from the Compact, are catalogued with their **origin** (the culture they come from) in place of a Domain. An origin is not a Gate environment, and no Gate has one.

A Gate normally has **one** Domain. A rare Gate has two, such as `Frozen + Constructed`, when that really defines the place.

### Everything from a Gate shares its Domain

> **G-03, the Red Forest · Verdant**
> - Redbark, Bellbone, Hunter's Moss: Verdant Resources
> - Redbacks, Bell-Throats: Verdant creatures

The Domain is shown on each Gate's page in the [Archive](archive.html) and on its Resources.

### What Domains are for

A Domain gives no bonus by itself. It's a shared keyword that other rules can refer to:

- a combat **Stunt** that works against creatures of one Domain;
- a researcher who **specializes** in Verdant Gates;
- **equipment** designed for Abyssal environments;
- a spellcaster who specializes in magic drawn from Frozen discoveries (see [Spellcasting](game.html#post-spellcasting));
- **Projects** that need a Resource of a particular Domain;
- **preparation**: knowing a Gate's Domain tells a crew what to bring (see [Expeditions](game.html#post-expeditions)).

That makes Domains useful to every kind of character, not only crafters.

### The Material Engineering Project

Combine or process existing Resources to produce a new material with the Functions you need. This is a normal Project (see [Projects](game.html#post-projects)) whose Goal is the new material.

> **Available:** Paleglass (`Dampen`, `Store`) · Vaultvine Fibre (`Flex`, `Reinforce`)
> **Needed:** `Store` + `Flex`
> **Project:** a flexible lining that holds its warmth for hours, for coats worn in Frozen Gates.

### Difficulty: how well do they combine?

The difficulty depends on how uncertain, dangerous or incompatible your proposed combination is. Start from how the Functions interact (see [Resource Functions](game.html#post-resource-functions)):

| Combination | What it means |
| --- | --- |
| **Synergy** | The Functions support each other (`Conduct` + `Store`). Comparatively easy. |
| **No known relationship** | Nobody knows yet. Moderate difficulty, and surprises are likely. |
| **Opposition** | The Functions work against each other (`Conduct` + `Dampen`). Hard, and it may need clever design, extra Requirements or a special Facility. |
| **Instability** | The Functions interact dangerously (`Catalyze` + `Release`). High risk, but controlling it can lead to advanced technology. |

Your approach matters too. A careful process in a proper workshop is less risky than combining samples in a field tent.

### When it goes wrong

Failed Material Engineering produces interesting Complications (see [Project Requirements & Complications](game.html#post-project-requirements-and-complications)), such as:

- the result is **unstable**;
- a **sample is lost**;
- a **dangerous by-product** appears;
- the material gains an **unexpected Function** or Special Property;
- you need a **Catalyst**;
- you need **specialized equipment**;
- you need an **expedition** to find what is missing.

### When it works

The new material gets:

- a **name**, chosen by the players who invented it, just as discoverers name the Resources they find (see [Resources](game.html#post-resources));
- its **known Functions**;
- its **origin**, meaning who made it, from what and where;
- any **Special Properties**, meaning unusual behavior it shows in the fiction.

It then counts as a Resource like any other and can meet Function Requirements.

### Experimental Substitution

You may suggest using a Resource whose Functions don't quite match a Requirement, as long as you can explain why it could work.

> *"It doesn't `Conduct`, but it does `Resonate`. If we tune the coil to its frequency, it should carry the charge."*

The GM may agree and, if so, may:

- increase the difficulty;
- add risk;
- change the properties of the finished item;
- require extra work.

Functions are meant to guide invention, not act as locks that only one key can open.

### Personal or Marketplace?

| | Result |
| --- | --- |
| **Personal Stash Project** | Makes **one** item for **one** character. See [Personal Stash & Crafting](game.html#post-personal-stash-and-crafting). |
| **Marketplace Project** | Makes that item **generally available** in Endros. |

### What a Marketplace Project may require

Making something reliably takes more than making it once. Typical Requirements (see [Project Requirements & Complications](game.html#post-project-requirements-and-complications)):

- a **working prototype**;
- Resources with particular **Functions** (see [Resource Functions](game.html#post-resource-functions));
- **Industry**, to manufacture it;
- **Commerce**, to source and distribute it;
- a **Facility** able to produce it;
- **specialist knowledge**;
- an **established supply** of a Resource, which can mean regular expeditions to a known Gate.

Industry and Commerce are the Capabilities most likely to support these Project Actions (see [Outpost Capabilities](game.html#post-outpost-capabilities)).

> **Example.** *Make Redbark Plate Available*: a Standard Public Project. It requires Ines's prototype, a steady supply of a Resource with `Regenerate` and `Reinforce` (Redbark is only Limited, so someone has to establish its supply) and the Workshops' presses. Harvesting Redbark in the Red Forest becomes a recurring job on the Job Board.

### When it's done

The item is added to the Marketplace and can be bought with Coins like any other Gear, at the price and availability the GM sets (see [Coins, Marketplace & Personal Stash](game.html#post-coins-marketplace-and-personal-stash)).

If producing the item needs a new Facility, start that Facility first. See [Facilities & Outpost Development](game.html#post-facilities-and-outpost-development).
